entire life has been one of subsistence  one of 12 children she remembers going  to bed with an empty stomach  my family struggled often we didn't have  enough food sometimes we ate once a day  sometimes nothing at all it was shameful  at times I went to bed hungry so my  younger siblings could eat  not much has changed Sylvia is eight  months pregnant and unemployed her  husband is employed as a bus assistant  but she says she struggles to feed  two-year-old Gail  his salary isn't enough for even one  person we spend the two hundred dollars  he makes on food and Necessities for my  son no clothes or anything else Silvia's  story is a familiar one among indigenous  families living high up in the Andes  region of Chimborazo  most make less than half the national  minimum wage of 480 dollars a month  Ecuador suffers from chronic levels of  malnutrition among children indigenous  communities in remote areas are  especially vulnerable  Charities say the covid-19 pandemic and  soaring inflation have worsened the  situation  four out of every ten children under the  age of two suffer from chronic  malnutrition and with it come growth  issues and learning issues this problem  is much worse in rural areas and we  believe the number has doubled since the  pandemic  the rising cost of living led to weeks  of protest by indigenous communities in  June  since then president Guillermo lasso has  pledged to spend 350 million dollars a  year to improve health care in basic  services  but 2023 is expected to be tough for  Ecuador as the government has imposed  austerity measures to tackle the budget  deficit giving families like Sylvia's  little hope of ending the Vicious Cycle  of hunger Alison rampieti Al Jazeera
 

